{
  "description": "MAINTAINER RULING #984, Q2, the cap: the queue holds AT MOST ONE pending invocation, and further intervals arriving while it is full COALESCE into it rather than accumulating (spec/interaction-events.md:235-242). Pinning a cap needs the run to stay OPEN after the blockage clears, which is the whole design of this fixture: a queued backlog is drained at a bounded rate -- at most one occurrence per tick dispatch and one per main-line statement boundary -- so if the main line stops supplying either the moment the block ends, ruling #984 closes the run and a capped and an uncapped queue are indistinguishable, both simply stopping. The measured trap: the same program with `wait 4` instead of `wait 26` prints twice under BOTH, and would have shipped as a cap fixture that pins no cap. Here a key press at tick 4 holds the execution thread for seventeen ticks while the `every 4` handler is claimed, and its intervals arrive on schedule at ticks 8, 12, 16 and 20 -- FOUR missed occurrences, all coalescing into the single slot. The outer `wait 26` then keeps the run open well past the blockage. Measured: SEVEN prints. Against a runtime whose queue is an uncapped counter the same program prints TEN -- exactly the three extra occurrences the cap discarded -- which is the unbounded buildup the sentence exists to forbid. Dropping the missed occurrence prints 6, and a fixed-delay clock prints 6.",
